---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "@/components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "@/components/Footer.astro";
---

<Layout
    title="About - Kevin Galligan"
    description="Kevin Galligan - native mobile developer, co-founder of Ntive Labs, previously Touchlab. New York, NY."
>
    <Header />
    <main>
        <section class="py-24 lg:py-32">
            <div class="container max-w-3xl">
                <div class="mb-16">
                    <h1
                        class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-6xl mb-6"
                    >
                        Kevin Galligan
                    </h1>
                    <p class="text-xl text-muted-foreground">
                        Native mobile developer. Mobile development efficiency
                        expert. Currently building AI tooling for mobile teams.
                        New York, NY.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div
                    class="prose prose-invert prose-lg max-w-none
                    prose-headings:font-bold prose-headings:tracking-tight
                    prose-h2:text-2xl prose-h2:mt-12 prose-h2:mb-4
                    prose-p:text-muted-foreground prose-p:leading-relaxed
                    prose-a:text-primary prose-a:no-underline hover:prose-a:underline
                    prose-ul:text-muted-foreground prose-li:my-1
                    prose-strong:text-foreground"
                >
                    <p>
                        I've been doing Android since before there were phones —
                        first clients in 2010, and Touchlab formally in 2011.
                        Since then my work has mostly been about one question:
                        how do you build two native mobile apps without doing
                        everything twice?
                    </p>

                    <h2>Now</h2>
                    <p>
                        Co-founder and CTO of <strong>Ntive Labs</strong>, with
                        the team that built Touchlab — native mobile since 2011,
                        AI-native since 2024. Our first product is
                        <strong>Parive</strong>, which uses layered AI analysis
                        to understand a pair of native iOS and Android apps and
                        align them concept by concept: every screen, feature,
                        and behavior, with a severity assigned to each
                        difference. It then coordinates planning and closing
                        those gaps.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        AI has changed how I work day to day. I barely write
                        code by hand anymore — I spend my time on structure,
                        context, and review, which turns out to be where the
                        judgment actually lives.
                    </p>

                    <h2>Before</h2>
                    <p>
                        I started <strong>Touchlab</strong> in 2011, when we
                        were New York's Android shop and nearly everyone else
                        was building iPhone apps. Over the years the work moved
                        from Android consulting into shared-code tooling for
                        native mobile teams, and I went all in on Kotlin
                        Multiplatform in 2018 — though the same idea goes back
                        further, to the J2ObjC work that preceded it.
                    </p>
                    <p>Some of what came out of that:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            The first Kotlin Multiplatform app in the App Store,
                            and the first iOS app shipping Compose
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>SKIE</strong>, which makes the Swift-facing
                            API generated from Kotlin considerably less painful
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Generics in Kotlin/Native's Objective-C output, plus
                            Xcode debugger and SPM publishing tooling
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            A long-running series of writing and talks on why
                            teams struggle to scale KMP — which turned out to be
                            about workflow and people more than tooling
                        </li>
                    </ul>

                    <h2>Community</h2>
                    <p>
                        I helped start <strong>droidcon New York</strong> and
                        ran the first three conferences, and ran the New York
                        Android meetup for a long stretch. I still speak
                        regularly — mostly about native mobile, Kotlin
                        Multiplatform, and lately about what AI does to mobile
                        development. Recent talks are on
                        <a href="/blog">the blog</a>.
                    </p>

                    <h2>Education</h2>
                    <p>Colgate University, 1994–1998.</p>

                    <h2>Elsewhere</h2>
                    <p>
                        <a
                            href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/kpgalligan/"
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noreferrer">LinkedIn</a
                        >
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>
    </main>
    <Footer />
</Layout>
